Wrapper around \code{\link[ecodive:beta_div]{ecodive::beta_div()}} and \code{\link[ecodive:alpha_div]{ecodive::alpha_div()}} providing
fast, C-accelerated alpha- and beta-diversity calculations directly on a
\code{phyloseq} object, plus support for \code{"robust_aitchison"} (alias
\code{"robust.aitchison"}).
}
\details{
\strong{Robust Aitchison distance (\code{metric = "robust_aitchison"}):}
Two definitions exist in the microbiome literature (both citing Martino et
al. 2019):
\itemize{
\item \strong{\code{impute = FALSE} (default, \code{ecodive} definition):} applies the robust
centered log-ratio (rCLR) transformation to strictly positive counts in
each sample (\code{log(x) - mean(log(x[x > 0]))} for \code{x > 0}, and \code{0}
otherwise) and computes the Euclidean distance via \code{ecodive}. This avoids
pseudocounts and runs in milliseconds in C, without iterative matrix
completion.
\item \strong{\code{impute = TRUE} (DEICODE / \code{vegan} OptSpace definition):} applies rCLR
leaving zeros as \code{NA}, completes the low-rank matrix with OptSpace
(\code{\link[vegan:decostand]{vegan::decostand()}} with \verb{method = "rclr", impute = TRUE}, called by
\code{vegan::vegdist(..., method = "robust.aitchison")}), and computes the
Euclidean distance on the completed matrix. Because \code{vegan} already
implements OptSpace matrix completion natively, no external \code{rpca}
dependency is needed.
}
